WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:08.243
A microphone in a healthy ear canal can record sound the cochlea itself made.

2
00:00:09.083 --> 00:00:10.083
Where does it come from?

3
00:00:10.783 --> 00:00:12.863
Start with a cochlea that has no help.

4
00:00:14.023 --> 00:00:16.963
Sound sets up a travelling wave along the basilar membrane.

5
00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:23.297
In a living ear, at soft levels, each place moves most for one frequency, its

6
00:00:23.297 --> 00:00:24.517
characteristic frequency.

7
00:00:25.417 --> 00:00:27.477
The wave peaks there and dies out just beyond.

8
00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:33.330
After death the wave remains, but weak and broadly tuned, and its peak can shift.

9
00:00:34.383 --> 00:00:35.723
Now pick one place.

10
00:00:35.723 --> 00:00:40.443
Play a tone at its characteristic frequency, and raise the level in steps.

11
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:45.703
After death, 10 dB more sound gives 10 dB more motion.

12
00:00:46.543 --> 00:00:47.763
That straight line is linear.

13
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:54.413
Predict. Where does the living ear differ most from the dead one, at soft levels or

14
00:00:54.413 --> 00:00:55.413
loud ones?

15
00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:06.533
At soft levels. A soft tone moves the living membrane tens of decibels more.

16
00:01:07.483 --> 00:01:13.443
As the tone gets louder, the living response grows slowly, often 2 to 5 dB for every

17
00:01:13.443 --> 00:01:14.443
10.

18
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:17.797
At the loudest levels, the two curves come close together.

19
00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:24.250
The gap between the curves is the cochlear amplifier.

20
00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.410
In mammals, it depends on outer hair cells.

21
00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:33.593
The gain is large only near the characteristic frequency, so it sharpens the peak as

22
00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:34.593
well as raising it.

23
00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:36.883
Here is the one idea.

24
00:01:37.963 --> 00:01:40.443
Outer hair cells add energy to the wave near its peak.

25
00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:46.387
The gain they give is large for soft sounds, small for loud ones, and gone after

26
00:01:46.387 --> 00:01:47.387
death.

27
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:52.670
Sensitivity, sharp tuning, compressive growth and the returning sound are four

28
00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:54.050
signatures of that one gain.

29
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.160
At the very softest levels, the gain stops rising.

30
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.420
It levels off at its maximum.

31
00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:03.153
Why want a gain that shrinks?

32
00:02:04.193 --> 00:02:07.373
Suppose the response grows 2.5 dB for every 10.

33
00:02:08.383 --> 00:02:14.383
If that slope held over 60 dB, a quiet room to a shout would fit into 15 dB of

34
00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:15.383
motion.

35
00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:20.633
That is a thousandfold range of sound pressure, fitted into less than a sixfold

36
00:02:20.633 --> 00:02:21.633
range of motion.

37
00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:26.777
Outer hair cells push with a motor protein in their side wall, called prestin.

38
00:02:27.757 --> 00:02:29.117
How does a cell push that fast?

39
00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:31.447
Take this with you.

40
00:02:31.447 --> 00:02:36.907
The living cochlea adds a gain that is large for soft sounds, and death erases it.
